WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:10.697
"Why I Changed My Mind on Water Fluoridation"

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:12.840
Community water fluoridation
recently hit

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.280
its 75th anniversary,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.460
touted as a significant
advancement in public health,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.860
but new research
suggests the potential

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.720
for adverse health impacts.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.600
There's little question
that supplemental fluoride

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.460
strengthens teeth
and reduces decay.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.900
But at what cost?

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.380
Most of the older studies
were snapshot-in-time studies,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.420
looking only at group-level data

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.760
or were based on
higher-than-normal exposure.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.520
That has since changed
with the publication

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:39.960
of prospective studies

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.180
following individual
exposure levels from

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.040
mother-child pairs over time.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.740
The first few were from Mexico

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.720
and correlated
higher fluoride levels

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.920
in the urine of pregnant women

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.920
with future developmental delay
in their infants.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.260
They found worse cognitive
performance at age four

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.600
and lower IQ
from ages six through 12,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.120
but the fluoride was
from fluoridated salt

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.940
or water sources where fluoride
was present naturally.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.000
Then, this study
came out of Canada,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.600
the first prospective study
using individual exposure data

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.200
dealing directly with
community water fluoridation

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.640
receiving quote-unquote

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.680
"optimally fluoridated" water.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.560
None of the municipal
water fluoride levels

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.540
exceeded 1.5 parts per million,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.550
and the vast majority,
over 90 percent,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.760
were under the U.S.
target of point seven.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.340
Despite drinking
"optimally fluoridated water,"

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.580
the study of more than
500 mother-child pairs

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.860
found that higher fluoride
exposure during pregnancy,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.080
once again,
appeared to translate

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.520
into lower child intelligence,
measured at ages three to four.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.760
The American College of
Obstetricians and Gynecologists

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.440
recommends pregnant women drink

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.060
at least eight cups
of water a day.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.680
Based on the Canadian study,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.000
following this advice

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.400
with water at U.S.
target fluoridation levels

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.820
might result in as much as
a 5-point loss in IQ.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.760
The Canadian study was published

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.220
in the American
Medical Association's

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.180
pediatrics journal,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.580
the longest running pediatrics
journal in the country.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.960
The editor-in-chief defended its
decision to publish the study

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.400
"as testament to the fact that
JAMA Pediatrics is committed

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.140
to disseminating
the best science...

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.800
regardless of how contentious
the results may be."

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.380
The Canadian Agency

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.940
for Drugs and Technologies
in Health

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.740
issued a rapid response report,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.960
concluding that there
is insufficient evidence

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.500
to conclusively conclude
that fluoride exposure

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.080
at optimum levels affects
neurological development,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.620
but that's not what
the Canadian study was saying.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.440
The researchers just suggested
the "possible need"

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.360
to reduce fluoride intake
during pregnancy.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.480
As the accompanying
editorial concluded,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.860
based on the new data,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.380
"a dispassionate
and tempered discussion

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.440
of fluoride's potential
neurotoxicity is warranted."

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.280
What was the response
from US regulators?

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.460
Within a month of
the Canadian study's release,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.200
the National Toxicology Program,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.180
which is part
of the US Department

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.200
of Health and Human Services

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.120
that evaluates substances

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.040
for potentially harmful
human health effects,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.160
released a draft evaluation
of fluoride exposure

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.860
and neurodevelopmental
and cognitive health effects

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.940
and concluded that,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.000
"Based on a systematic review
of the evidence,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.800
the NTP concludes that
fluoride is presumed to be

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.400
a cognitive neurodevelopmental
hazard to humans."

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.300
So we've gone
from dismissing people

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.100
who questioned the safety
of water fluoridation

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.500
as zealots
or anti-science fanatics,

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.220
to the official US National
Toxicology Program saying

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.840
we should presume
it's hazardous.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.780
Now, this was just
a draft report.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.120
To ensure the integrity
of its report,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.100
the NTP asked
the National Academies,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.500
probably our most prestigious
scientific institution,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.820
to review it.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.040
The review raised
a bunch of concerns,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.800
which was portrayed
in the dental literature

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.960
as "discrediting"
the IQ research.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.900
But that's not what
the National Academies said.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.960
They explicitly said that
their misgivings don't mean

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.220
that the NTP conclusion

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.580
that fluoride should be presumed
to be neurotoxic is incorrect;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.980
they just wanted
some further analysis.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.160
So, in 2020,
they redrafted their draft,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.320
and once again came
to the same conclusion,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.560
presuming it's a cognitive
neurodevelopmental hazard

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.840
based on the extent,
consistency,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.180
and robustness of the IQ data.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.740
The National Academies

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.020
then rereviewed
their redrafted draft,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.760
concluding it's much improved,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.860
but urged the NTP
to further improve

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:32.940
the clarity of the document,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.760
and we await
the final NTP report.

